  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 15,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.467,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.059
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sound Waves: Characteristics and Applications"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 199"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.776,
            0.225
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can humans hear all sounds? Humans can only hear a limited range of sound frequencies. The audible range or the human hearing range is from 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz (20 kHz). However, this range varies from person to person and decreases with age. Sound waves with frequency below 20 Hz are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "infrasonic waves",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", while sound waves with frequency above 20 kHz are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ultrasonic waves",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Humans cannot hear infrasound or ultrasound but some animals can. Dogs, cats, bats, dolphins can detect ultrasound, while elephants can detect infrasound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.233,
            0.215,
            0.25
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Loudness"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.253,
            0.775,
            0.322
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Humans perceive the amplitude of a sound wave as loudness. Sounds with larger amplitude are heard louder, while those with smaller amplitude sound softer. The loudness decreases as we move farther away from the source."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.325,
            0.774,
            0.377
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In everyday language, loudness and intensity are often used interchangeably. However, intensity is a measurable quantity, whereas loudness depends on the listener’s hearing ability."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.379,
            0.162,
            0.433
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.096,
                0.379,
                0.162,
                0.433
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.394,
            0.476,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.43,
            0.814,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sound loudness is commonly measured in decibels (dB). Very soft sounds like rustling leaves are around a few dB, normal conversation is about 60 dB, while very loud sounds, such as firecrackers can exceed 100 dB. Even a small increase in the dB level means a large increase in the sound intensity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.495,
            0.815,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Unwanted or harmful sound is called noise. Noise pollution is a severe problem. Exposure to sound levels above recommended limits, especially for long durations can affect health, sleep, and hearing. Prolonged exposure to loud sound can lead to hearing loss, which can be tested using audiograms. People with hearing loss may use hearing aids, which consist of a microphone, amplifier, and speaker to help the wearer hear and communicate better."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.085,
            0.615,
            0.151,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.085,
                0.615,
                0.151,
                0.681
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.64,
            0.365,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.673,
            0.581,
            0.767
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We often do not realise how amazing our sense of hearing is. When sound enters the ear, it causes a thin membrane called the eardrum to vibrate (Fig. 10.24). Tiny bones quickly amplify these vibrations and the cochlea converts them into electrical signals that rush to the brain, which perceives them as sound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.77,
            0.58,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Having two ears allows the brain to pinpoint the direction of the origin of sound. By comparing which ear hears the sound first, it figures out where the sound came from based on the tiny time gap between the two (often less than a thousandth of a second)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.85,
            0.58,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Animals hear in different ways. Snakes and fish sense vibrations through their bodies, while some insects have ear-like organs on their body parts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.595,
            0.711,
